  "summary" : "The openloop project's `graph.html` lets users build a tree of connected node \"cells\" defining a knowledge flow / knowledge graph. On it user can define knowledge flow, by creating a tree of connected nodes (represented as cells on the screen).\n\nThen nodes become knowledge context for \"RUNs\" - user can hit \"Run\" on a node and pick-up from a node's point in time, with all prior knowledge.\n\nFurthermore user may continue on a run by focusing a \"RUN\" and asking more follow-up questions.\n\nNodes can also have \"personas\" and (remote) MCP configuration assigned/attached to them. Then when \"persona\" runs as part of a runner's workflow, or user performs a \"RUN\" on a node's cell - MCP & summaries context is passed to `yolo` agent.\nEssentially this makes nodes a tree-like attachable knowledge & configuration graph - that is why it is called graph.html.\n\ngraph.html has 2 core modes when showing cells: normal and bird's eye view modes. And a right-side view/panel that reveal properties of the node when it's cell is selected, plus it allows to follow-up on \"RUNs\" (continue conversation).\nWhen adding new (or removing) features to/from `graph.html` it is important to consider how feature shall be working in various modes and views.",
  "prompt" : "This is `openloop` project, it has `graph.html`. On it user can define knowledge flow, by creating a tree of connected nodes (represented as cells on the screen).\n\nThen nodes become knowledge context for \"RUNs\" - user can hit \"Run\" on a node and pick-up from a node's point in time, with all prior knowledge.\n\nFurthermore user may continue on a run by focusing a \"RUN\" and asking more follow-up questions.",
